the fantastical trinity of american  childhood  santa claus the easter bunny and the  tooth fairy  as children we're brought up to believe  in these magical gift givers  in hopes of instilling a sense of wonder  and a sense of morality in us  children generally stop believing in  these mythical figures at around the age  of seven or eight  but why do we believe in them in the  first place  where do these traditions come from and  when did they arise in our collective  consciousness  in this video we will explore these very  questions  [Music]  santa claus is a large jolly old man  with a big white beard and a red coat  he lives in the north pole has a  workshop full of elves  a pack of flying reindeer and every  christmas eve he flies all across the  world  giving presents to children he seems  like quite the character  but how did he come to be most of us  know that he's also called saint nick  so what he's a christian saint he seems  quite different from all the other  saints we know  well he is in part the modern santa  claus is an amalgamation  of four different characters from  european history and folklore  saint nicholas sinterklaas father  christmas  and the pagan god odin saint nicholas  was an actual historical person  he lived in the 4th century and was the  bishop of myra  which is in modern-day turkey not much  is actually known about the historical  saint  but there are many stories of him  performing miracles  as well as secretly giving gifts which  is where santa claus's penchant for gift  giving comes from  saint nicholas however did not give  gifts to children he mostly gave money  to the poor  and those in need the most famous story  of his generosity is the story of the  three  dowries in which saint nicholas secretly  paid the dowries of three young women  so that they could be married and not  fall to prostitution  because of saint nicholas's famed  generosity  a figure arose in the netherlands called  sinterklaus  sinterklaus is essentially based  entirely on saint nicholas  and there is little magic surrounding  him like there is with the modern santa  claus  on december 6 the feast day of saint  nicholas  sinterklaas rides through the streets on  a white horse  dressed in his clerical garb and throws  candy and cookies to the children  he is joined by zvart pete a chimney  sweep  who punishes bad children by hitting  them with his broom  zvert pete has come under fire in recent  years for  his association with blackface  traditionally the dutch public  denied that the figure was racist but in  the last few years the opinion has  shifted  there have been efforts recently to have  the people playing pete  simply be covered in soot and not  actually  painted black another pre-santa figure  comes from jolly old england  and his name is father christmas  nowadays father christmas  is essentially synonymous with santa  claus and some british people may not  even know that they are different but  traditionally  they're not the same father christmas is  the personification of christmas  he's represented as an old man wearing  green with a wreath of holly on his head  you may recognize him as the ghost of  christmas present  from charles dickens is a christmas  carol unlike  santa he's more interested in mary  making and drinking wine than actually  giving presents to children  the tradition started in the 15th  century and had a resurgence in the 17th  century as a backlash against the  english puritans who had outlawed  christmas  seeing it as catholic and pagan and  against christian values the puritans  may have actually had a point in calling  the tradition pagan  as it is now theorized that father  christmas may have been based  at least in part of the germanic god  odin  odin was the father of the gods norse  mythology  and there is evidence that he or a god  similar to him by the name of loden  was worshiped by the english  anglo-saxons before they were  christianized  he was associated with the midwinter  festival of yule  a holiday that has been absorbed into  modern day christmas  so basically santa claus is a  combination of the english  father christmas who may have been based  on odin  and the dutch sinterklaas who comes from  saint nicholas  but that only explains a few parts of  the mythology  like the gift giving the general look  and his name  what about the rest the reindeer the  elves the north pole  well those are modern or at least more  modern editions  the reindeer are first mentioned in the  famous poem  a visit from saint nicholas also known  as a night before christmas  by clement clark moore in fact many  parts of the santa claus myth were  either created by  or recorded by that poem it is also  partly responsible for the christmas  elves  in the poem santa himself is described  as an elf for how else would he fit down  the chimney  lastly the north pole the idea that  santa lives in the north pole  started from illustrations done by the  artist thomas nass  at the beginning of the last century for  the magazine harbors weekly  during this time arctic exploration was  all the rage  so nast thought it would be fun to put  santa  up there too  [Music]  on to the bunny the easter bunny is a  rabbit that comes every easter  to give colorful eggs and candy to  children the tradition was started  by german lutherans in the 16th century  originally the easter bunny was similar  to santa in the sense that it only gave  eggs and candy to good children  though the morality tale seems to have  stopped in modern times  but the question arises why a bunny  and why eggs i mean rabbits don't lay  eggs do they  well both rabbits and eggs were seen in  pagan cultures as signs of fertility and  birth and were symbols of springtime the  germanic goddess oester  also called ostara for whom easter gets  its name  had a festival in the spring as well  which over time  morphed into the christian holiday her  symbols were the rabbit and eggs  though it's important to note that not a  whole lot is known about oester  and the information we have about her  comes from later christian writers  so the connections may not be as  straightforward as they seem  rabbits and eggs also had specifically  christian symbolism as well  bunnies ironically were a symbol of  purity  it was believed because rabbits  reproduce so much  that they might actually be  hermaphroditic and  were able to reproduce asexually because  of this they were associated with the  virgin mary  who was said to have bore jesus before  having sex  eggs were used as a symbol of christ's  resurrection  the egg shell was seen as a tomb and the  chick that came out of the tomb  was like christ rising from the dead  eggs were usually dyed red to represent  the blood of christ  unlike santa claus and the easter bunny  whose origins are  relatively clear the tooth fairy is more  of a mystery  the tooth fairy is a fairy usually  depicted as female  who comes and takes the baby teeth of  children and leaves some money behind  his compensation  it is a much more recent phenomenon  starting  in the early 20th century and only  becoming widespread in the 1920s and 30s  superstitions about baby teeth and how  to dispose of them have gone back  centuries however  and exist in almost every culture in the  world common rituals  tend to fall into two camps ones where  the child  throws the teeth somewhere like in a  fire  onto the ground or into the air and ones  where the teeth are left for something  like a dog or most commonly a mouse  in much of europe it is customary to  leave the tooth  under a pillow for a mouse to come and  take it these  mouse myths predate the tooth fairy so  it's fair to say that the tooth fairy  came from this myth so why did it change  to a fairy  and where did the money exchange even  come from in the early to mid 20th  century  the motif of the good fairy had become  popular in american culture  due to movies like the wizard of oz and  disney movies like 