sed  entirely on saint nicholas  and there is little magic surrounding  him like there is with the modern santa  claus  on december 6 the feast day of saint  nicholas  sinterklaas rides through the streets on  a white horse  dressed in his clerical garb and throws  candy and cookies to the children  he is joined by zvart pete a chimney  sweep  who punishes bad children by hitting  them with his broom  zvert pete has come under fire in recent  years for  his association with blackface  traditionally the dutch public  denied that the figure was racist but in  the last few years the opinion has  shifted  there have been efforts recently to have  the people playing pete  simply be covered in soot and not  actually  painted black another pre-santa figure  comes from jolly old england  and his name is father christmas  nowadays father christmas  is essentially synonymous with santa  claus and some british people may not  even know that they are different but  traditionally  they're not the same father christmas is  the personification of christmas  he's represented as an old man wearing  green with a wreath of holly on his head  you may recognize him as the ghost of  christmas present  from charles dickens is a christmas  carol unlike  santa he's more interested in mary  making and drinking wine than actually  giving presents to children  the tradition started in the 15th  century and had a resurgence in the 17th  century as a backlash against the  english puritans who had outlawed  christmas  seeing it as catholic and pagan and  against christian values the puritans  may have actually had a point in calling  the tradition pagan  as it is now theorized that father  christmas may have been based  at least in part of the germanic god  odin  odin was the father of the gods norse  mythology  and there is evidence that he or a god  similar to him by the name of loden  was worshiped by the english  anglo-saxons before they were  christianized  he was associated with the midwinter  festival of yule  a holiday that has been absorbed into  modern day christmas  so basically santa claus is a  combination of the english  father christmas who may have been based  on odin  and the dutch sinterklaas who comes from  saint nicholas  but that only explains a few parts of  the mythology  like the gift giving the general look  and his name  what about the rest the reindeer the  elves the north pole  well those are modern or at least more  modern editions  the reindeer are first mentioned in the  famous poem  a visit from saint nicholas also known  as a night before christmas  by clement clark moore in fact many  parts of the santa claus myth were  either created by  or recorded by that poem it is also  partly responsible for the christmas  elves  in the poem santa himself is described  as an elf for how else would he fit down  the chimney  lastly the north pole the idea that  santa lives in the north pole  started from illustrations done by the  artist thomas nass  at the beginning of the last century for  the magazine harbors weekly  during this time arctic exploration was  all the rage  so nast thought it would be fun to put  santa  up there too  [Music]  on to the bunny the easter bunny is a  rabbit that comes every easter  to give colorful eggs and candy to  children the tradition was started  by german lutherans in the 16th century  originally the easter bunny was similar  to santa in the sense that it only gave  eggs and candy to good children  though the morality tale seems to have  stopped in modern times  but the question arises why a bunny  and why eggs i mean rabbits don't lay  eggs do they  well both rabbits and eggs were seen in  pagan cultures as signs of fertility and  birth and were symbols of springtime the  germanic goddess oester  also called ostara for whom easter gets  its name  had a festival in the spring as well  which over time  morphed into the christian holiday her  symbols were the rabbit and eggs  though it's important to 